| Sources and culture | ma-g6-sources (unit 4, days 49 to 62) | ma-g7-sources (unit 8): strong example works guide the making; media builds knowledge in learning | ma-g8-sources (unit 12) retrieves ma-g7-sources (unit 8, days 113 to 126): cultural and social knowledge; local to worldwide events | Sources weighed and history as the setting, then model works and learning, then culture and the wider world |

### Handed on to Media Arts, grades 9 to 12

| This course hands on | Unit here | Row that picks it up (Level 1) | What Level 1 does with it |
|---|---|---|---|
| ma-g8-read | 9 | ma-g9-read | Artists' choices read and more than one sense considered |
| ma-g8-society | 9 | ma-g9-society | Power, equality, identity and digital identity enter the study |
| ma-g8-ideas | 10 | ma-g9-ideas | Named idea methods and written aesthetic standards |
| ma-g8-craft | 10 | ma-g9-craft | Roles chosen by the student, and creative risk taken on purpose |
| ma-g8-make | 11 | ma-g9-make | Deliberate choices of emphasis and tone tied to the student's own goals |
| ma-g8-judge | 11 | ma-g9-judge | Evaluation at key production stages, not only on the finished piece |
| ma-g8-present | 12 | ma-g9-present | A collection, not a single piece, designed for varied audiences |
| ma-g8-sources | 12 | ma-g9-sources | Outside and own sources woven into one original piece |

Each hand-on above is answered at both ends in data/progressions/arts.csv (the Level 1 rows' `assumes` cells).
Level 1 is entered by level, not grade (STANDARD.md 3.2): a student new to media arts starts there, and its bridge
unit (at most 12 days) teaches the grade 8 techniques and words it needs as readiness, claiming no grade 8 id, so
nothing in Level 1 requires this course.

## Models carried

| Model | Where it comes from | Introduced or first used here | Used again here |
|---|---|---|---|
| look-describe-interpret | From grade 5 (ma-g5-read, model_out; ma-g6-read, model_in) | Unit 1: retrieved, then written as an analysis with a reason from the piece | Unit 5 (qualities compared, taste weighed, ma-g7-read), unit 9 (styles contrasted, ma-g8-read); every unit's study of a work as review |
| media-ethics-check | From grade 5 (ma-g5-society; ma-g6-society, model_in) | Unit 1: the class copyright and ethics card, checked on every source of the investigation piece | Unit 5 (the class's responsible-use rules, ma-g7-society), unit 9 (legal, technical and virtual-world limits, ma-g8-society); every later unit's credits as review |
| storyboard | From grade 5 (ma-g5-ideas; ma-g6-ideas, model_in) | Unit 2: plans, mockups and production steps in a planning portfolio critiqued against the purpose | Unit 6 (a prototype and a resource check, ma-g7-ideas), unit 10 (a whole workflow, ma-g8-ideas); units 3, 7 and 11 make from it |
| production-roles | From grade 5 (ma-g5-craft; ma-g6-craft, model_in) | Unit 2: the role cards (director, framer, sound, editor, continuity) and the role record, roles assigned | Unit 6 (roles across, with organizer, ma-g7-craft), unit 10 (a range of skills, ma-g8-craft); every team piece as review |
| revision-log | From grade 5 (ma-g5-make; ma-g6-make, model_in) | Unit 3: the revision log, each change and why it serves the effect and the audience | Unit 7 (stressing elements for audience and setting, ma-g7-make), unit 11 (technical polish, ma-g8-make) |
| critique-protocol | From grade 5 (ma-g5-judge; ma-g6-judge, model_in) | Units 1 and 2 use its describe, ask, suggest routine as each unit's one critique (APPROACH "Critique"), taught from the critique card to a student new to it; unit 3 extends it with given, then chosen, criteria | Unit 7 (criteria the student builds, ma-g7-judge), unit 11 (criteria weighed against goals and context, ma-g8-judge); every unit's critique as review |
| screening-plan | From grade 5 (ma-g5-present; ma-g6-present, model_in) | Unit 4: the showing plan comparing formats, and the written review after the screening | Unit 8 (a format chosen by evaluation, ma-g7-present), unit 12 (several formats, an improvement put in place, ma-g8-present) |
| source-log | From grade 5 (ma-g5-sources; ma-g6-sources, model_in) | Unit 4: the source log, each source judged for quality and credited | Unit 8 (strong example works, ma-g7-sources), unit 12 (cultural and social knowledge, ma-g8-sources); every credit line from unit 1 on uses its short form as review |

All eight go on to Level 1 (ma-g9 rows' model_in).

## Units

| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Processes, anchors and works | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | Reading the frame. *How do the parts of a media piece build its meaning, and how does media tie to who people are and where they come from?* | 14 | ma-g6-read; ma-g6-society | MA:Re7.1.6a; MA:Re7.1.6b; MA:Cn11.1.6a; MA:Cn11.1.6b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Louis and Auguste Lumi�re, Workers Leaving the Lumi�re Factory (Lyon, France, 1895, public domain), a record of one moment; Segundo de Chom�n, The Electric Hotel (El hotel el�ctrico) (Spain, 1908, public domain), a staged fantasy in which luggage and objects move by stop tricks; Zacharias Kunuk (Inuk, of Igloolik, born 1957; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Qaggiq (Gathering Place) (Canada, 1989), a video made with Igloolik Inuit that restages a 1930s winter gathering to keep its skills and ways seen, described and linked to the maker's own production company page, never embedded; Dadasaheb Phalke, Raja Harishchandra (India, 1913, public domain; the surviving reels), India's first feature film, entertainment drawn from an epic story | Retrieval in day 2's opener: telling two pieces apart (ma-g5-read) and the class ethics checklist (ma-g5-society), with the ten-minute reteach for a student new to media arts. MA:Re7.1.6a: name and describe the parts of a piece (shots, frames, sound, titles, order) and write how they build its meaning. MA:Re7.1.6b: name techniques and styles (a fixed long take, a stop trick, drawn frames, staged scenes) and analyze how they steer the audience. MA:Cn11.1.6a: investigate how media ties to identity, history, entertainment and community, in a short made piece (a captioned photo sequence, a one-minute audio essay or a poster) on one tie, with a public, invented or class-shared subject offered beside a personal one and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.6b: analyze media tools and spaces and use them with fair use, copyright and ethics: the class copyright and ethics card, every source in the piece credited and checked against it; fair use is explained, not relied on. Technique: shot sizes (long, medium, close) and the fixed frame against the staged frame. Words: part, meaning, technique, style, identity, copyright. Showing: the investigation pieces shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.6a, a written item: an unseen public domain clip's parts and how they build its meaning, with a reason from the piece; MA:Re7.1.6b, a written item: one technique in the same clip and how it steers the viewer; MA:Cn11.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept investigation piece: one tie of media to identity, history, entertainment or community shown with evidence; MA:Cn11.1.6b, a kept card: the media-ethics check on the piece, every source credited and its use checked against the card. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: contrast across styles (grade 8), personal taste in the analysis (grade 7). Tools: shot-size step card, work cards, the class copyright and ethics card, Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed stills. Free path: printed stills, a card viewfinder, paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First because every later unit needs the reading routine and the copyright and ethics card (the look-describe-interpret and media-ethics-check models are retrieved here, where first needed this year). Reading opens the year so a student studies how media is built before planning one; the investigation piece is short so the production roles wait for unit 2 | Candidate: a check of each work's flash and content against the safety rules at the excerpt the unit plan names | Kunuk's video was made with Inuit of Igloolik, Nunavut, about their own past: the sensitivity agent reads the maker's own production company page and his published words first; the unit studies it as Inuit telling their own history, and no student imitates Inuit dress, song or games. Raja Harishchandra tells a Hindu epic story: studied as a film and its setting, never as belief taught or judged; the agent reads an Indian film archive's or museum's page first. Identity ties: a student never has to tell their own identity; the public, invented or class-shared option is marked the same. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, no strobe or flashing excerpt, recording only with the family's yes, devices wiped and handed back. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 6 | Plan, test, produce. *How do media makers plan and test a piece, and what does each job on a team add?* | 18 | ma-g6-ideas; ma-g6-craft | MA:Cr1.1.6; MA:Cr2.1.6; MA:Pr5.1.6a; MA:Pr5.1.6b; MA:Pr5.1.6c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Winsor McCay, Gertie the Dinosaur (United States, 1914, public domain), a drawn animation made frame by frame in the ordinary way; Norman McLaren and Evelyn Lambart, Begone Dull Care (Canada, 1949, the national film board's free viewing page, linked), pictures painted and scratched straight onto film, an experimental use of the tool; the class's own team pieces | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a proposal tested for an audience (ma-g5-ideas) and roles and conventions (ma-g5-craft). MA:Cr1.1.6: use a chosen creative method (a mind map, a "what if" list or a constraint game) to set several aims and fixes for one media project. MA:Cr2.1.6: organize plans, a mockup and the production steps in a planning portfolio and critique each against the purpose. MA:Pr5.1.6a: perform an assigned production role (director, framer, sound, editor, continuity) and build its skills, logged on the role record. MA:Pr5.1.6b: test a constraint on purpose (30 seconds, one location, no spoken words) to find solutions. MA:Pr5.1.6c: use a tool both in the ordinary way and in an experimental way (frame-by-frame drawing against drawing or scratching across the strip). Technique: a 12-drawing flip book or a stop-motion sequence from a storyboard (move a little, capture, move again). Words: method, aim, mockup, role, constraint, experimental. Showing: each team's 30-second piece screened to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.6, a guide look-for on the kept planning portfolio: several aims and fixes from a named method; MA:Cr2.1.6, a guide look-for on the same portfolio: each plan and the mockup critiqued against the purpose; MA:Pr5.1.6a, a kept card: the role record, the assigned role's tasks done and one skill built; MA:Pr5.1.6b, a written item: a new constraint card, two solutions and the one chosen, why; MA:Pr5.1.6c, a written item: a tool from the tray named in one ordinary and one experimental use, and what each gives. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a prototype judged for resources (grade 7). Tools: the planning portfolio, role cards, storyboard sheets, a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper flip books, a paper film strip, found objects. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 1 because planning a piece needs the parts and techniques just named and the ethics card for any source used; before unit 3 because a point-of-view piece is made from a plan and in roles. The storyboard and production-roles models are retrieved here. Longest unit of the year: five ids, a planning portfolio and a team production | Candidate: a living animator from Africa or Asia beside McCay and McLaren, described and linked, for the year's range | Begone Dull Care changes color fast: the unit plan checks its excerpt against the flash rule (success criterion 2.3.1) and, if it fails, shows still frames and describes the motion. Team work: a student who cannot work in a team holds every role in turn on a solo piece, marked the same. Hazards and controls: lamps low-power and kept off paper, cords taped, blades by an adult only, recording only with the family's yes, screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 6 | Point of view. *How does where the camera stands change what a viewer feels, and how do we judge a piece fairly?* | 14 | ma-g6-make; ma-g6-judge | MA:Cr3.1.6a; MA:Cr3.1.6b; MA:Re8.1.6; MA:Re9.1.6 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Dziga Vertov, Man with a Movie Camera (made in Kyiv, Kharkiv and Odesa, then the Soviet Union, now Ukraine, 1929, public domain in the United States), many angles and a camera that is seen; Yasujiro Ozu, Tokyo Story (Japan, 1953), a camera held low and still, at the height of someone seated on the floor, described and linked to a film archive's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: deciding changes for clarity (ma-g5-make) and criteria set by the student (ma-g5-judge). MA:Cr3.1.6a: try several approaches (high, low and eye-level angle; a point-of-view shot) to make content with a point of view. MA:Cr3.1.6b: judge how changing one element (angle, shot size, order, sound) would reach the intended effect, then refine the piece for its audience, logged on the revision log. MA:Re8.1.6: analyze what different pieces intend, using criteria given on the criteria card. MA:Re9.1.6: choose criteria of the student's own to judge pieces and their production steps, and give constructive feedback. Technique: camera angle and point of view, the same scene framed three ways. Words: point of view, angle, approach, intent, criteria, feedback. Showing: a three-to-five-shot point-of-view sequence (photographed, or drawn as a storyboard of finished panels) shown to a partner class or the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept sequence: at least two approaches tried, one point of view held; MA:Cr3.1.6b, a kept card: the revision log, one element changed for effect and the audience refinement; MA:Re8.1.6, a written item: what an unseen piece intends, judged on the given criteria; MA:Re9.1.6, a written item: criteria the student chooses, used to judge a classmate's piece (shared with permission) and its production steps, with one constructive suggestion. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: narrative structure (grade 7). Tools: the angle step card, the criteria card, the revision log, Curio Class's own. Free path: a card viewfinder and drawn panels. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because the sequence is planned with the storyboard and made in the roles just learned; before unit 4 because the screening joins finished work. The revision-log model is retrieved here and the critique-protocol model gains its given and chosen criteria | Candidate: a living maker known for point-of-view shots, from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Vertov's film was made to show Soviet city life; the unit names its setting in one plain sentence and the agent reads a film archive's page first. The unit plan names its excerpts and leaves out the film's childbirth and bathing scenes. Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only pieces they have permission to share in class. Hazards and controls: filming only in school spaces with the teacher's leave and of things and hands unless a family has said yes; no climbing for a high angle (a high angle is taken from a standing height or drawn); screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 6 | Then and now: our screening. *How can media give an event from the past a new meaning, and how did our screening go?* | 14 | ma-g6-present; ma-g6-sources | MA:Pr4.1.6; MA:Pr6.1.6a; MA:Pr6.1.6b; MA:Cn10.1.6a; MA:Cn10.1.6b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10). Works: Lewis Hine's photographs of child workers for the National Child Labor Committee (United States, 1908 to 1916, the Library of Congress, public domain), with their captions, which helped change the laws; Dawoud Bey (born 1953; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), The Birmingham Project (2012), paired portraits of Birmingham children and adults the ages the bombing's victims were and would have been, described and linked to a museum's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: two formats weighed (ma-g5-present) and sources gathered (ma-g5-sources). MA:Pr4.1.6: blend forms (still pictures, voice, text, sound) in one 60-to-90-second piece and show how each backs its core message. MA:Pr6.1.6a: analyze showing formats (a class screening, a looping hallway screen, a printed photo-essay wall) and do varied tasks to present and share the piece inside the school, on the showing plan. MA:Pr6.1.6b: analyze how the screening went and what to improve, in a written review. MA:Cn10.1.6a: find, judge and draw on resources, inner (what the student knows and wonders) and outer (research in public domain archives), to make the piece, on the source log. MA:Cn10.1.6b: show how media gives a historical event new meaning, in the piece itself: a "then and now" on an event the student researches from public domain sources (a public or class-chosen event offered beside a family one, marked the same). Technique: a narrated photo sequence, voice and sound timed to the pictures. Words: format, core message, blend, resource, research, review. Showing: the class screening of the year, to a partner class or families with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.6, a look-for marked at the screening: every form backs the core message; MA:Pr6.1.6a, a kept card: the showing plan comparing two formats, with the tasks done to present; MA:Pr6.1.6b, a written item on the second check day: how the screening went and one improvement, with evidence from the audience; MA:Cn10.1.6a, a kept card: the source log, each source judged for quality and credited, research included; MA:Cn10.1.6b, a written item on the second check day: how one unseen public domain photograph took on a new meaning when it was used later. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a format chosen by evaluation (grade 7). Tools: the showing plan, the source log, a work viewer, Curio Class's own; public domain archive pages opened by the teacher. Free path: a paper photo-essay wall and a live-voiced picture sequence. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the year because the screening joins the year's making, framing and sound into one piece; the screening-plan and source-log models are retrieved here, where first needed. Its screening and written check end the year's units before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: an archive set from outside the United States on a historical event, for range | Hine photographed real children, named in his captions: the unit studies them with respect and their setting, and no student plays a child worker. Bey's project remembers the four girls killed and two boys killed that day in Birmingham, Alabama, 15 September 1963: the unit tells the event in one plain sentence from the Birmingham Civil Rights Institute's and the museum's published words, read first by the sensitivity agent, shows no image of the bombing, and gives no role play. A student's chosen event: no family or personal history is required, and no event of violence to people is chosen without the teacher's check. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, volume low, recording only with the family's yes, family yes for any showing outside class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 7 | Taste and technique. *How do technique and taste shape what we feel about a piece, and what is media for in people's lives and jobs?* | 14 | ma-g7-read; ma-g7-society | MA:Re7.1.7a; MA:Re7.1.7b; MA:Cn11.1.7a; MA:Cn11.1.7b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Alice Guy-Blach�, Falling Leaves (United States, 1912, public domain, a Library of Congress copy), a short drama by a French-born maker who ran her own film studio, studied with her career; Ousmane Semb�ne, Borom Sarret (Senegal, 1963), a day of a cart driver in Dakar, described and linked to a film archive's page; an invented social platform feed written by Curio Class (invented for practice) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the written analysis (ma-g6-read) and the copyright and ethics card (ma-g6-society). MA:Re7.1.7a: compare the qualities of parts of two pieces (shot length, movement, sound, setting) and how they relate. MA:Re7.1.7b: analyze how technique and style join with personal taste to shape the experience, the student's taste named and weighed against evidence from the piece. MA:Cn11.1.7a: explore what one kind of media is for and the values it carries (a job in media from the federal labor statistics agency's free career pages, as of the unit plan's date; a social platform from the invented feed), in a short made piece (an explainer poster, a one-minute audio piece or a captioned sequence). MA:Cn11.1.7b: analyze media tools and spaces and use them responsibly, social platforms included, by writing the class's rules for responsible use from the invented feed. Technique: the long take against the cut, timed and compared. Words: quality, relate, taste, purpose, value, responsible. Showing: the explainer pieces shown to the class beside the class rules. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.7a, a written item: the qualities of one part in each of two unseen clips and how they relate; MA:Re7.1.7b, a written item: how one technique and the student's taste shape the experience of an unseen clip, with evidence; MA:Cn11.1.7a, a guide look-for on the kept explainer: what the media is for and one value it carries, with evidence; MA:Cn11.1.7b, a kept card: the student's own lines of the class rules applied to three posts of the invented feed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: styles contrasted (grade 8); legal and virtual-world limits (grade 8). Tools: the invented feed on paper, media career cards (Curio Class's own, from the labor statistics agency's pages), work cards, the rules card. Free path: the printed feed and paper explainers. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First in year 7 because it reopens the reading routine and the ethics card after the summer and moves the analysis to comparing two pieces with taste weighed; careers and platforms come before the year's productions so the class rules govern them | Candidate: a media maker's own published account of their job, for the career card | Social platforms: no student account is opened or named, no student is asked which platforms they use, and the feed is invented; the leave-out line offers a school notice board instead. Borom Sarret shows a poor man's day under a city's rules: the agent reads a film archive's page and Semb�ne's own published words first. Careers: no job is ranked by worth. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, no strobe, recording only with the family's yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 7 | Prototype it. *How does a prototype show a team whether a plan will work with what it has?* | 18 | ma-g7-ideas; ma-g7-craft | MA:Cr1.1.7; MA:Cr2.1.7; MA:Pr5.1.7a; MA:Pr5.1.7b; MA:Pr5.1.7c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Chris Marker, La Jet�e (France, 1962), a film told almost wholly in still photographs, described and linked to a film archive's page: a tool for stills adapted to tell a moving story; Sky Hopinka (Ho-Chunk Nation, born 1984; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), J�aji Approx. (2015), a short film that sets the filmmaker's recordings of his father beside words in the Ho-Chunk language, described and linked to the artist's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: plans critiqued against purpose (ma-g6-ideas) and assigned roles and tested constraints (ma-g6-craft). MA:Cr1.1.7: use an inventive process such as prototyping (a quick paper or photographed animatic) to produce varied ideas and solutions. MA:Cr2.1.7: draft and judge plans, mockups and production steps for the expressive aim and the resources on hand (the resource check: time, space, devices or none, people). MA:Pr5.1.7a: show growing skill across roles, including solving problems and organizing (the organizer's schedule role joins the role cards). MA:Pr5.1.7b: explore while building solutions, logged as tries on the role record. MA:Pr5.1.7c: adapt tools and methods, ordinary and experimental, to an assigned purpose: a 45-second public notice for the school (a library hour, a safe hallway, a club). Technique: the animatic, stills timed to sound to test a plan before production. Words: prototype, resource, organize, explore, adapt, purpose. Showing: each team's public notice screened to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.7, a guide look-for on the kept planning portfolio: varied ideas from a prototype; MA:Cr2.1.7, a guide look-for on the portfolio: plans judged against the aim and the resource check; MA:Pr5.1.7a, a kept card: the role record across two roles, one organizing task done; MA:Pr5.1.7b, a written item: one problem the team met and two solutions explored; MA:Pr5.1.7c, a written item: a new assigned purpose card, and how the student would adapt one tool and one method to it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a whole workflow (grade 8); content adapted to the student's own intent (grade 8). Tools: the planning portfolio, role cards, the resource check card, a frame-sequence viewer or its printed animatic strip, Curio Class's own. Free path: a paper animatic and a live-voiced notice. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 5 because the notice follows the class rules and its purpose is a media purpose just studied; before unit 7 because the story piece is made from a prototype, by teams that can organize. Longest unit of the year: five ids and a team production | Candidate: a third prototype-led work from Asia, described and linked | Hopinka's film carries the Ho-Chunk language and his family: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own published words and a Ho-Chunk Nation language page first; no student imitates or uses Ho-Chunk words. La Jet�e ends with a death: the unit plan names the excerpts and tells the ending in one plain sentence. Team work: a solo version holds every role in turn. Hazards and controls: lamps low-power and kept off paper, cords taped, recording only with the family's yes, screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
