Each check day is a written sitting: set-up 3 minutes, self-check 5, the written items 25, collecting and clean-up 5,
with no device out but the teacher's screen for the unseen piece. Every student's evidence is collected once in a
window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days (year 7 starts on day 65, year 8 on day 129).
On the calendar, each mid-year check falls on school day 83 and its respond day on 85; each year-end check on school
day 158 and its respond day on 160.

## Cross-subject

### English language arts, grades 6 to 8 (same year)

| Used here | Row and lean | Earliest unit of the other course | If later |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 3 (year 6, course days 35 to 48, about school days 88 to 120): critique talk about what pieces mean and whether they work | ma-g6-judge leans on ela:SL.6.1 (class talk) | english language arts grade 6 unit 1 (days 1 to 32) owns SL.6.1 | Not needed in the usual order; if a school's ELA runs late, the critique card states the talk rules (come prepared, follow the rules, ask and answer, review what was said) and the teacher models them on the first critique day |

Practiced, not owned: every written analysis practices claim and evidence writing (W.6.1 to W.8.1), and unit 7
sits beside english language arts grade 7 unit 4 (days 87 to 108), whose text-against-film work (RL.7.7) uses the
same shot words; neither course waits on the other.

### Computer science (not a core course)

ma-g8-society leans on computer-science:g8-attribution (when credit is owed for copied or remixed media). Computer
science is not a core course (lesson 32), so nothing here assumes it: as STANDARD.md 6 says, unit 1 teaches the
copyright and credit step its piece needs and unit 9 adds the license line itself, in short steps, and notes the
computer science grade 8 attribution row as where the idea goes deeper. Its 6 to 8 frame is not cited here.

### Mathematics and social studies, grades 6 to 8

No lean. Frame counts (12 drawings for a second, 30 or 60 seconds of running time) use whole numbers only (lesson
13). Works are taught with their time and place on work cards (bar criterion 7) with social studies' sourcing habits
(who made it, when, for whom), which the units teach themselves; unit 4's historical events are researched in public
domain archives, never by role play.

## For the lead

1. **Media arts connecting ids swapped (settled), as in the K to 2 frame.** Checked at source (the California standards page for
   MA:Cn10 grade 3): anchor 10 is the student's own experience and anchor 11 society, culture and history, as in
   every other discipline. The words-file lines for MA:Cn10.1.* and MA:Cn11.1.* were swapped, every ma-*-sources
   row now carries the Cn10 ids and every ma-*-society row the Cn11 ids, and this frame's id labels changed to match
   (the pairs of rows stay in their units).
2. **Media arts weekly time figure, middle school.** STANDARD.md 3.4 has no media arts figure; the visual and media
   arts association's middle school checklist (arteducators.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/OTL-Middle_School_Visual_Media_Arts_Program.pdf)
   is read at source (a download, allowed by Desk q-fix-downloads-1, but not by this job), then STANDARD.md 3.4
   gains a row headed "Media arts, 6 to 8" and the Pending note in the Days bullet is replaced with the figure and
   the gaps on both paths.
3. **After-core check days.** Mid-year checks on school day 83 (week 17) in grades 6, 7 and 8, respond day 85;
   year-end checks on school day 158, respond day 160 (week 32, finals). Day 83 holds no core check in any of the
   three grades; health and physical education 6 to 8 hold theirs on day 84, a different school day, so stagger rule
   3 is kept. Left for the lead's one calendar pass. The once-a-week path's checks are in weeks 17 and 31.
4. **Paper path meets the tool, polish and platform ids (item-level, decided).** As the K to 2 media arts frame's
   item 5: "media tool" is read as anything that frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a
   device-free class meets MA:Pr5.1.6c to 8c and MA:Cr3.1.8b in full on paper or live, and MA:Cn11.1.7b and 8b
   (social platforms, virtual worlds) through invented case cards with no account. If the lead or Cory wants the
   technical-quality id met only on a device, the course needs one shared device per team of five or six, which is
   a cost question.
5. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $40 (year 6), $40 (year 7), $45 (year 8) a class of 30,
   all optional beside a free path. Pick: the same ceiling as the visual arts pilot's pick, $75 a class a year.
6. **Works to confirm at source.** Several picks rest on memory for dates and holding pages (Kunuk's Qaggiq, 1989,
   and its holding page; Guy-Blach�'s Falling Leaves, 1912; Smith's The Birth of a Flower, 1910; Ray's Two, 1964; Hopinka's J�aji Approx., 2015; Diamond's Reel Injun,
   2009; Vyarawalla's independence photographs, 1947; de Chom�n's The Electric Hotel, 1908). Each unit plan opens the item's own page for
   the rights line and corrects the date or swaps the work under the frame's rule; none needs a download or a
   sign-in.

