ssion) with one constructive suggestion. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: style conventions and unity (grade 8). Tools: the shot-list sheet, the criteria builder card, the revision log, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn panels and a live sound track. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because the story is planned from a prototype by teams that organize; before unit 8 because the explainer screening merges content into one story with one viewpoint | Candidate: a living storyteller in film from the Americas, described and linked | Two shows a rich boy and a poor boy and toy guns: the unit names the setting in one plain sentence, and the agent reads a film archive's page and Ray's own published words first. The Kid shows a child taken from his home by officials: the teacher tells the scene's setting briefly; no role play. Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Hazards and controls: filming only in school spaces with the teacher's leave, recording only with the family's yes, screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
290: | 8 | 7 | Media that teaches. *How has media made new knowledge, and how do we choose the best way to show what we learned?* | 14 | ma-g7-present; ma-g7-sources | MA:Pr4.1.7; MA:Pr6.1.7a; MA:Pr6.1.7b; MA:Cn10.1.7a; MA:Cn10.1.7b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10). Works: Eadweard Muybridge, The Horse in Motion (California, United States, 1878, the Library of Congress, public domain), photographs that showed for the first time how a horse's legs move; F. Percy Smith, The Birth of a Flower (United Kingdom, 1910, public domain, a film archive's open copy), time-lapse film that showed flowers opening, a growth too slow for the eye | Retrieval in day 2's opener: formats analyzed (ma-g6-present) and sources weighed (ma-g6-sources). MA:Pr4.1.7: merge content types (stills, motion, voice, text, sound) into one explainer with one steady viewpoint and story. MA:Pr6.1.7a: evaluate showing formats against the audience and choose one, then do the presenting tasks. MA:Pr6.1.7b: evaluate the results and improvements after the screening, weighing the student's own growth since unit 4. MA:Cn10.1.7a: find, judge and use varied sources, strong model works among them, to guide the explainer, on the source log. MA:Cn10.1.7b: explain how media builds new meaning and knowledge, in learning: how the two works made knowledge that words alone had not. The explainer teaches one thing that moves or grows (a public, science or class topic; the student's own topic offered beside it and marked the same). Technique: a motion sequence slowed down (stills in order) with a voice that explains. Words: viewpoint, merge, evaluate, model work, knowledge, growth. Showing: the explainer screening for a partner class or families with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.7, a look-for marked at the screening: content types merged with one viewpoint and story; MA:Pr6.1.7a, a kept card: the showing plan evaluating two formats and the choice made; MA:Pr6.1.7b, a written item on the second check day: the results, one improvement and the student's own growth since unit 4, with evidence; MA:Cn10.1.7a, a kept card: the source log, varied sources judged, a model work named and how it guided the piece; MA:Cn10.1.7b, a written item on the second check day: how one unseen public domain science image or clip made new knowledge. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: one theme in several formats (grade 8). Tools: the showing plan, the source log, a frame-sequence viewer or its printed strip, Curio Class's own. Free path: a printed still sequence and a live voice. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in year 7 because the explainer joins the year's prototype, story and sound into one screened piece; the sources thread moves from history to learning. Its screening and written check end the year's units before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: a science or teaching film by a maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked, for range | The explainer's topic is checked by the teacher against the values rule; no topic about a student's own body or health. Muybridge's horse studies are fine; the unit plan picks plates with no human figures. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, volume low, recording only with the family's yes, family yes for any showing outside class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
291: | 9 | 8 | Media and the values we share. *How do different styles make an audience feel, and how does media serve civic and environmental values?* | 14 | ma-g8-read; ma-g8-society | MA:Re7.1.8a; MA:Re7.1.8b; MA:Cn11.1.8a; MA:Cn11.1.8b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Gordon Parks, American Gothic, Washington, D.C. (1942, the Library of Congress, public domain), a portrait of Ella Watson, a government cleaner, made to show segregation; William Anders, Earthrise (1968, the federal space agency's image library, public domain), the Earth seen from lunar orbit, a photograph tied to the environmental movement; Neil Diamond (Cree, of Waskaganish, Quebec), Reel Injun (Canada, 2009), a documentary on how films have shown Native people, described and linked to the film board's or maker's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: comparing qualities with taste (ma-g7-read) and the class's responsible-use rules (ma-g7-society). MA:Re7.1.8a: contrast the qualities of, and links among, the parts and styles within two pieces (a documentary portrait and a space photograph; a documentary that quotes old films). MA:Re7.1.8b: analyze and contrast how techniques and styles steer what audiences feel and are meant to think. MA:Cn11.1.8a: explain and show, in a short made piece, how media links to civic, environmental or community values and purposes (a school or public value offered beside a personal one, marked the same). MA:Cn11.1.8b: analyze media tools within legal (copyright, open licenses, public domain), technical and virtual-world contexts, and use them responsibly: the media-ethics check gains a license line, and two invented case cards (a virtual world and a shared-image tool) are analyzed. Technique: style named by its conventions (documentary, portrait, science image). Words: contrast, style, civic, environmental, license, virtual. Showing: the values pieces shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.8a, a written item: the parts and styles of two unseen public domain pieces contrasted, with links named; MA:Re7.1.8b, a written item: how one technique in each steers what the audience feels; MA:Cn11.1.8a, a guide look-for on the kept values piece: one civic, environmental or community value shown with evidence; MA:Cn11.1.8b, a kept card: the media-ethics check with every source's license or public domain status, and one invented case card analyzed for responsible use. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: power, equality and digital identity (Level 1). Tools: work cards, the license card, two invented case cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed stills, paper pieces. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First in year 8 because it reopens reading and the ethics card after the summer and adds the legal setting every later piece needs; contrast of styles comes before the year's style-blending unit (11) | Candidate: a civic or environmental media piece by a living maker from Asia, described and linked | Parks's portrait shows Ella Watson in segregated Washington: the agent reads the Library of Congress page and the Gordon Parks Foundation's published words first, and the unit tells her name and setting in plain sentences. Reel Injun quotes films that show Native people as stereotypes: the unit describes, never screens, those clips, and the agent reads the maker's and the film board's pages first; no student plays a Native character. Values: no student takes or argues a side on a current political question; the values rule holds. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, recording only with the family's yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

