290:| 9 | 5 | Selling and telling. *How do two pieces steer us differently, and how has advertising shaped a community over time?* | 16 | ma-g5-read; ma-g5-society | MA:Re7.1.5a; MA:Re7.1.5b; MA:Cn11.1.5a; MA:Cn11.1.5b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through a poster's eye path (review). Works: advertising trade cards (United States, 1870s to 1890s, public domain, a public library's open digital collection), small picture cards that shops handed out; the unit plan picks cards free of the racial and ethnic caricatures common in this form; hand-painted film posters from Ghana's traveling video shows (Ghana, about 1985 to the 2000s; painters such as Daniel Anum Jasper; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date, and picks a poster free of violence or horror), described and linked to a museum's or exhibition's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's explained parts and techniques (ma-g4-read, unit 5) and the ethics card (ma-g4-society, unit 5). MA:Re7.1.5a: name, describe and tell apart how the parts of two pieces build message and meaning (a trade card beside a painted poster). MA:Re7.1.5b: name, describe and tell apart how their techniques and styles steer the audience. MA:Cn11.1.5a: investigate how media ties into life and community, including advertising, its history and its ethics (what an ad leaves out; who is shown and who is not), in a short piece: an ad for an invented product with a caption on what it leaves out. MA:Cn11.1.5b: examine and discuss the class's tools and spaces and keep the class checklist for ethical, media-literate use (who made this, why, what is left out, may I use it). Technique: a poster or title card with emphasis by size, color and placement (the eye path). Words: tell apart, steer, advertising, history, ethics, media literacy. Showing: the invented-ad wall with its "what it leaves out" captions. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.5a, a written item on two unseen pieces: how their parts build different messages; MA:Re7.1.5b, a talk item: one technique in each and how they steer differently; MA:Cn11.1.5a, a guide look-for on the kept ad and caption: one ad technique used, what it leaves out, and one fact of advertising's history from the work cards; MA:Cn11.1.5b, a kept card: the class checklist applied to one unseen piece. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: news coverage (unit 12). Tools: the media-literacy checklist, the eye-path step card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap card and markers. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in year 5 because it reopens the reading routine and the tool rules (now media literacy) after the summer, and moves reading to two pieces told apart. 16 days, two more than years 3 and 4, because the history of advertising adds a study with its own work cards. Unit 12's news study builds on its checklist | Candidate: an advertising work from Asia, open access, for range | Trade cards: many carry caricatures of African American, Chinese American, Irish American and Native people; the sensitivity agent checks each card picked and the library's page; if a student asks why such cards existed, the teacher names the harm plainly in one line from the library's own words and moves on. Ghana posters: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's or exhibition's page and the painters' own published words first. Ads are for invented products only, with no brand names; no task asks what a family buys or wants. Hazards and controls: markers labeled non-toxic. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
291:| 10 | 5 | Bend the rules. *How do makers draw ideas from other artists' work, and what happens when a tool is used in an experimental way?* | 18 | ma-g5-ideas; ma-g5-craft | MA:Cr1.1.5; MA:Cr2.1.5; MA:Pr5.1.5a; MA:Pr5.1.5b; MA:Pr5.1.5c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Te Wei with a team of animators, Where Is Mama (Xiao kedou zhao mama) (Shanghai, China, 1960), an animated film made in the style of ink-wash painting, tadpoles looking for their mother, described and linked; PES (Adam Pesapane), Western Spaghetti (United States, 2008; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), object stop-motion in which everyday things stand in for food, described and linked to the maker's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's goal and combined plans (ma-g4-ideas, unit 6) and rotating roles and design steps (ma-g4-craft, unit 6). MA:Cr1.1.5: imagine new ideas from the student's own experience or another artist's work (the two films), credited. MA:Cr2.1.5: develop and test a proposal and a model (a short test strip or clip) with the goal and a stated audience in mind (a grade 2 class, families). MA:Pr5.1.5a: practice varied roles that build skills in art, design, technology and teamwork. MA:Pr5.1.5b: bend a usual convention on purpose to solve a problem in the project (an object standing in for another, a sound that does not match the picture on purpose). MA:Pr5.1.5c: study ordinary and experimental ways a tool can serve (a brush for outlines and a brush for wash; the viewfinder or device held still and moved). Technique: object stop-motion with stand-in objects, and wash animation on paper (each frame painted in thin washable ink or watercolor). Words: imagine, proposal, audience, convention, ordinary, experimental. Showing: the test films or strips for the stated audience. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.5, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: ideas drawn from the student's experience or a named artist's work, credited; MA:Cr2.1.5, a guide look-for on the storyboard: a proposal naming the audience, and a model tested with its result; MA:Pr5.1.5a, a kept card: the role record, two roles and one skill each in art, design, technology or teamwork; MA:Pr5.1.5b, a guide look-for on the piece: a convention bent on purpose, named in the student's line; MA:Pr5.1.5c, a written item: one tool's ordinary and experimental use compared. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an assigned role and tested constraints (grade 6). Tools: the storyboard sheet, the proposal card, role cards and the object stop-motion step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: found objects, drawn frame strips, water and a brush. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 9 because a proposal for an audience uses its reading of how pieces steer an audience. Longest in year 5: five ids, two techniques and the varied roles. Before unit 11, whose emphasis and clarity work refines a piece the crew can now capture | Candidate: an experimental animation by a living maker from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Ideas drawn from another artist's work are credited by name. Hazards and controls: no food, glass or sharp objects as stop-motion objects (allergies, waste, cuts); ink and paint labeled non-toxic and washable; hands washed; water cups on a tray; the device on a stand. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
292:| 11 | 5 | Make it clear. *How does emphasis make a meaning clear, and how do my reading and the group's compare?* | 12 | ma-g5-make; ma-g5-judge | MA:Cr3.1.5a; MA:Cr3.1.5b; MA:Re8.1.5; MA:Re9.1.5 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Buster Keaton and Edward F. Cline, One Week (United States, 1920, public domain), a silent comedy in which a newly married couple build a kit house that goes wrong; Abbas Kiarostami, Bread and Alley (Nan va kucheh) (Tehran, Iran, 1970; Kiarostami 1940 to 2016), a short film of a boy carrying bread home past a dog in an alley, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's arranged and refined piece (ma-g4-make, unit 7) and criteria on production steps (ma-g4-judge, unit 7). MA:Cr3.1.5a: make content and join its components (shots, title cards, sound) to express a meaning, using emphasis (a cut-in close-up, a held shot, a sound cue). MA:Cr3.1.5b: decide how elements could change for clearer communication, then refine for clarity (the revision log names the decision before the change). MA:Re8.1.5: compare the student's reading of a piece with the group's, weighing its aim and context. MA:Re9.1.5: set criteria and use them to evaluate pieces and production steps, giving kind, useful feedback that names a look-for. Technique: the cut (shot order, the cut-in close-up, continuity) in a six- to ten-shot sequence, drawn on a shot strip and cut and reordered with scissors and tape, or captured on a shared device. Words: join, emphasis, clarity, refine, compare, feedback. Showing: the sequences at a class viewing. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.5a, a guide look-for on the finished sequence: components joined to a stated meaning, with emphasis; MA:Cr3.1.5b, a guide look-for on the revision log: the decision for clarity written before the refinement, and the change made; MA:Re8.1.5, a written item on an unseen piece: the student's reading beside the group's (from the class chart), with its aim and one fact of its context; MA:Re9.1.5, a kept card: the student's own criteria and written feedback to a classmate that names a look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several approaches tried for a point of view (grade 6). Tools: the shot-strip template, the revision log, the criteria card and the feedback card, Curio Class's own; a shot-strip editor or the printed strip. Free path: scrap paper, scissors and tape. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | After unit 10's crew and capture skills; the revision log and critique card return one step harder (the change decided first; the student's own criteria). Before unit 12, whose newscast needs clear emphasis and the habit of comparing readings | Candidate: the group-reading chart tried as a paper version for one adult at home | Keaton's comedy uses falls and stunts: no student re-enacts a stunt or a fall. Kiarostami's film has a barking dog that frightens the boy: a student afraid of dogs is told before viewing and may study stills instead. Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Hazards and controls: blunt-tip scissors; screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
293:| 12 | 5 | Our newscast. *How do news and community events shape what people remember, and which format tells our story best?* | 14 | ma-g5-present; ma-g5-sources | MA:Pr4.1.5; MA:Pr6.1.5a; MA:Pr6.1.5b; MA:Cn10.1.5a; MA:Cn10.1.5b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: José Guadalupe Posada, a news broadside (Mexico City, 1890s to 1910s, public domain, the Library of Congress or a museum's open collection; the unit plan picks a sheet on a non-violent event, not a calavera); a Lakota winter count from the Smithsonian's online exhibit "Lakota Winter Counts" (National Anthropological Archives; the unit plan picks the count), in which each year's main event is kept as one picture; a newsreel story of a community event (United States, 1930s to 1960s, the National Archives, public domain; the unit plan picks a fair, a parade or a school event, never a disaster or a war) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's coordinated piece and public showing (ma-g4-present, unit 8) and research with sources (ma-g4-sources, unit 8). MA:Pr4.1.5: make a piece that blends different kinds of content: a class newscast (an anchor, a report, an interview, a title card or a picture chart). MA:Pr6.1.5a: weigh what two showing formats offer (a live newscast in a box frame before an audience, against a recorded take or a printed news sheet) and fulfill a role in presenting. MA:Pr6.1.5b: compare the two formats' results and ways to improve. MA:Cn10.1.5a: gather and use the student's knowledge, interests, experiences and outside sources (a school event, an interview with a consenting adult at school, a library source), on the source log; a public or invented story is marked the same. MA:Cn10.1.5b: show how news coverage or a community event shapes people's cultural experience (what a broadside, a winter count and a newsreel each kept of an event, and what people remember because of it). Words: newscast, format, compare, gather, news, community event. Showing: the newscast for another class or families, with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the newscast is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.5, a look-for marked at the showing: kinds of content blended into one newscast; MA:Pr6.1.5a, the kept screening-plan card (two formats weighed), scored with a look-for on the role carried out; MA:Pr6.1.5b, a talk item: the two formats' results compared and one improvement; MA:Cn10.1.5a, the kept source log: the sources gathered, credited and used; MA:Cn10.1.5b, a written item: how one of the three works shaped what people remember of an event. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: formats analyzed against a core message, with a written review (grade 6). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, the newscast run sheet and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a box frame, a printed news sheet. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the band because the newscast blends the year's reading of ads and news (unit 9), its crew and capture (unit 10) and its emphasis for clarity (unit 11), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the source thread then goes to grade 6's weighed sources and history | Candidate: the winter count's source confirmed as made with Lakota keepers, from the exhibit's own credits | Lakota winter count: the sensitivity agent first studies the Smithsonian exhibit, made with Lakota keepers, and a Lakota-run source; it is studied by looking only; no student makes a winter count or uses Lakota pictographs, and the class's own year record uses its own form. Posada: no calaveras, crime or execution sheets. The newscast reports school or invented events; no student reports on a classmate's family or a private matter; interviews are with consenting adults at school, recorded only with the family's yes; a student may take an off-camera role (sound, picture chart, floor manager), and the printed news sheet keeps every id met (leave-out line). Hazards and controls: cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level; no flashing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
