303:| 1 | Level 1 | The bridge: frames, shots and a crew. *What does a media maker need to know before the first real piece?* | 12 | | None | Bridge unit, readiness only (STANDARD.md 3.2): creating, producing and responding at the grade 8 step. Works: Georges Méliès, A Trip to the Moon (France, 1902, public domain; the Library of Congress holds a copy), studied for shots, sequence and the crew behind early film | Readiness check in the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook: shot names, a three-panel storyboard, the critique card's three steps, two production roles. Then, as readiness and claiming no grade 8 id: the shot set (wide, medium, close, high and low angle) with a card viewfinder; a six-shot sequence planned on the storyboard and workflow sheet and captured by drawing or a shared device; the production role cards; the revision log; the critique card (describe, ask, suggest); the media-ethics check card and the source log; the screening-plan card. Words: shot, angle, sequence, workflow, role, credit. Showing: the six-shot sequences screened in class, unscored. Evidence: the readiness check and a bridge record (each card used once), unscored, so the bridge's days count as days outside the level's standards (12 of 160, 7.5 percent, within the bar's 10 percent). Tools: step cards, the shot-call card set, all eight model cards, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed strip. Free path: card viewfinders, paper, pencils. Short path: 6 days | First because Level 1 assumes no earlier media arts learning and every later unit uses the eight models (STANDARD.md 3.2: Level 1 opens with a bridge of at most 12 days). Short because it teaches only the grade 8 tools Level 1 needs, at their first step | Candidate: whether the readiness check needs a picture-only form for a student new to English | Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped and handed back by the device card; cords taped down; card cut by an adult; no filming of anyone without their yes. Méliès's film is studied as a 1902 French work, with the setting told. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
304:| 2 | Level 1 | Who gets shown, and how. *How do media makers' choices give or take power, and what does that mean for the people in the frame?* | 30 | ma-g9-read; ma-g9-society | MA:Re7.1.Ia; MA:Re7.1.Ib; MA:Cn11.1.Ia; MA:Cn11.1.Ib | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); producing practiced in the made piece. Works: photographs from Edward S. Curtis, The North American Indian (United States, 1907 to 1930, public domain; the Library of Congress and Northwestern University's digital edition), made for a survey that sometimes staged its subjects; Cara Romero (Chemehuevi, born 1977; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), TV Indians (2017), a staged color photograph answering how screens have shown Native people, described and linked to the artist's own page; Sebastião Salgado (Brazil; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), photographs of the Serra Pelada gold mine (Brazil, 1986), described and linked, on labor and who looks at whom | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's shot set and the look-describe-interpret steps. MA:Re7.1.Ia: analyze how parts (frame, light, pose, caption), style and preferences show up in the pieces and in the makers' choices (what Curtis left out of his frames; what Romero put in). MA:Re7.1.Ib: analyze how a piece steers what an audience experiences through more than one sense, on a public domain film clip played with sound on and with sound off, and on a photograph sequence with and without its captions. MA:Cn11.1.Ia: explain and show how media ties to power, equality, social trends and identity, in a made six-image sequence with captions (photographed, drawn or cut from public domain images) titled "Who gets shown?", about a place or group the student chooses, an invented community or a public subject marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.Ib: evaluate the legal (consent, copyright, license), technical and career sides of media work, digital identity included, on Curio Class case cards: an invented young photographer's profile, a release form, a license, a job posting. Words: frame, caption, gaze, representation, consent, digital identity. Showing: the "Who gets shown?" sequences on the class wall or screen. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.Ia, a mid-unit sitting item (after the study days): parts, style and the maker's choices in an unseen public domain photograph sequence; MA:Re7.1.Ib, a sitting item on the end check day: how an unseen public domain clip steers experience through sight and sound, played twice; MA:Cn11.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the kept sequence: a link between media and power, equality, a trend or identity, shown in the images and named in the captions; MA:Cn11.1.Ib, the kept media-ethics check on the student's own sequence plus a written evaluation of one case card's legal, technical and career sides, digital identity included. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: propaganda and commerce (Level 2). Tools: work cards, case cards, the media-ethics check card, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed images. Free path: public domain images printed, paper, scissors, tape. Short path: 15 days | After the bridge because analysis uses its shot words and look-describe-interpret steps; first of the level's paired units because reading media and its power comes before making for an audience, and unit 3's ideas start from the questions raised here. The media-ethics check grows here to digital identity before any team production in unit 3 | Candidate: a Curio Class original clip as the unseen work, so its rights are certain | Native representation: the sensitivity agent first reads the Chemehuevi Indian Tribe's own pages, the artist's own statement on TV Indians and the Library of Congress pages on Curtis; the unit picks Curtis plates that do not show a ceremony, says plainly that some of his images were staged and that some show ceremonial dress, studies them by looking only, and no student restages or imitates them (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). Salgado's mine photographs show hard labor: the agent reads the holder's page; no student is asked about their own family's work. Identity tasks offer an invented or public option marked the same; no student's own profile or account is used. Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped; scissors; no filming of anyone without their yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
305:| 3 | Level 1 | Moon shots: ideas and a crew. *How do media makers find an idea and a way to make it, and what does a risk buy them?* | 34 | ma-g9-ideas; ma-g9-craft | MA:Cr1.1.I; MA:Cr2.1.I; MA:Pr5.1.Ia; MA:Pr5.1.Ib; MA:Pr5.1.Ic | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Georges Méliès, A Trip to the Moon (1902) from unit 1, revisited for its tricks (stop and substitute, painted sets); William Kentridge (South Africa, born 1955; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Journey to the Moon (2003), made in his studio from charcoal, a coffee pot and a camera, described and linked to a museum's or the artist's page; Norman McLaren and Evelyn Lambart, Begone Dull Care (Canada, 1949, the National Film Board of Canada, free on its own site, linked, never embedded), painted and scratched straight onto film | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's storyboard and workflow sheet and the role cards. MA:Cr1.1.I: use two named idea methods (a "what if" list and a forced pairing of two unrelated things) to form many ideas, set a goal and solve a problem, in a planning portfolio. MA:Cr2.1.I: write a short list of three to five aesthetic standards for the piece and use them to refine the plan and a prototype around a named inspiration (one of the works), the goal and the setting where it will be shown. MA:Pr5.1.Ia: choose a production role in a team of five and carry it out, logging growth in one art, design, technical or people skill with evidence. MA:Pr5.1.Ib: take a creative risk on purpose to meet a set constraint (one location, no spoken words, twelve shots at most), logged with what it cost and gained. MA:Pr5.1.Ic: combine a usual and an innovative tool, method or material to carry the intent (erase-and-redraw charcoal photographed frame by frame, cutouts on a lit sheet, marks scratched or drawn on a paper strip). The piece: a 60- to 90-second team "moon shot" film, or its paper form, a drawn strip screened through the frame-sequence viewer or as a flip book. Words: idea method, goal, aesthetic standard, prototype, risk, constraint. Showing: the moon shots screened for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.I, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio, scored at the mid-unit checkpoint: two named methods used, many ideas, a goal set and a problem solved; MA:Cr2.1.I, a guide look-for on the portfolio and the two prototypes: the aesthetic standards written and the second prototype refined against them, with inspiration, goal and setting named; MA:Pr5.1.Ia, the kept production log: the chosen role carried out and one skill's growth shown with evidence; MA:Pr5.1.Ib, a guide look-for on the finished piece with its log entry: the risk taken and the constraint met; MA:Pr5.1.Ic, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student shows the combined tool or method on one frame and says the intent it carries), with the piece's look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: managing a whole production and interactivity (Level 2). Tools: the idea-method cards, the aesthetic standards sheet, role cards and the production log, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed strip. Free path: charcoal or soft pencil, paper, a lamp or window light. Short path: 17 days | After unit 2 because its questions about who is shown feed the ideas, and the media-ethics check is in place before a team captures anything. Before unit 4 because a piece refined to the student's own goals needs a prototype and the habit of a log first. The longest unit before mid-year: five ids and the level's first team production | Candidate: a Latin American or Asian maker of a hand-made animation, described and linked, for range | Hazards and controls: charcoal dust (damp wipes, no blowing dust, hands washed); lamps low-power, kept off paper; cords taped; shared devices wiped; card cut by an adult. Kentridge's films often concern apartheid; this film is chosen for its studio process, and the sensitivity agent reads the holder's page and the artist's published words before the setting is told. Team work: a student who cannot work in a team holds every role in turn on a solo piece. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
306:| 4 | Level 1 | Tone and emphasis. *How does a maker make a piece feel the way they mean it to, and how do we judge it while it is still being made?* | 38 | ma-g9-make; ma-g9-judge | MA:Cr3.1.Ia; MA:Cr3.1.Ib; MA:Re8.1.I; MA:Re9.1.I | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Yasujirō Ozu, Tokyo Story (Japan, 1953), a still, low camera and long pauses that carry a family's distance, one scene described and linked to a film archive's page; Wanuri Kahiu (Kenya, born 1980; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Pumzi (2009), a short film whose color and sound set the tone of a dry future, described and linked; a public domain newsreel from the Library of Congress, studied for how it was received in its own time on a Curio Class reception sheet | Retrieval in day 2's opener: unit 3's aesthetic standards and the revision log. MA:Cr3.1.Ia: make deliberate choices organizing content and style with emphasis and tone (what is big, long, loud, held; what is cut), in a 2-minute image-and-sound piece, a twelve-image photo essay with a sound bed, or a paper strip with a live sound score. MA:Cr3.1.Ib: refine aesthetic quality and accent style elements to match the student's own stated goals, logged change by change. MA:Re8.1.I: analyze how a piece is meant and how it was received, in a personal setting and a cultural setting, using the reception sheet (who saw it, where, what was said). MA:Re9.1.I: evaluate the student's own work at two key production stages (the rough cut and the final) against identified criteria, weighing context and aims. Words: emphasis, tone, pacing, sound bed, reception, criteria. Showing: the pieces in a class screening. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the finished piece: two deliberate choices of emphasis or tone, named in the log; MA:Cr3.1.Ib, the kept revision log: two refinements, each tied to a stated personal goal; MA:Re8.1.I, a sitting item on the end check day: how an unseen public domain piece is meant and how its reception sheet shows it was received, in a personal and a cultural setting; MA:Re9.1.I, two kept written critiques of the student's own piece, the first at the rough cut (the mid-unit assessment) and the second at the final, each against named criteria with context and aims weighed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: refining for a chosen audience's impact (Level 2). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, reception sheets, Curio Class's own; a work viewer. Free path: paper strip, found-sound objects for a live score. Short path: 19 days | After mid-year and unit 3 because tone and emphasis are worked on a piece of the student's own, after a team prototype taught the log and standards. Before unit 5 because the collection needs finished pieces, and judging at production stages comes before judging a showing | Candidate: a sound-only work (a public domain radio piece) beside the two films, for the sound sense | Tokyo Story is about aging parents and grief: the unit plan names the scene shown; grief is studied in the film, never asked of a student. Pumzi's setting is a future without water: the sensitivity agent reads the maker's own published words. Personal goals and taste are the student's own; a choice of subject is never marked down. Hazards and controls: headphones low; screen breaks; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
307:| 5 | Level 1 | A collection for two audiences. *How can media weave many sources and forms into one showing, and how does media build culture?* | 40 | ma-g9-present; ma-g9-sources | MA:Pr4.1.I; MA:Pr6.1.Ia; MA:Pr6.1.Ib; MA:Cn10.1.Ia; MA:Cn10.1.Ib | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating in the blended piece. Works: the National Museum of the American Indian's Native Knowledge 360° online teaching pages (Smithsonian, United States, living Native educators and makers), studied as media that builds culture through online learning, linked; public domain films from the Prelinger Archives and the Library of Congress, as remix sources; Kahiu's Pumzi from unit 4, revisited for how a film travels to festivals, schools and screens | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's screening-plan card and source log. MA:Pr4.1.I: blend arts and media forms (image, sound, live voice or music, text, movement) into one unified team piece, planning for audience reaction and interaction (a planned moment the audience answers, votes or joins). MA:Pr6.1.Ia: design how the class collection is presented and distributed for two audiences and formats (a screening for a younger class and an evening for families in the school's space, with a printed program). MA:Pr6.1.Ib: judge the first showing and act on an improvement for the second, weighing gains to the maker and the community. MA:Cn10.1.Ia: find, judge and weave outside sources (public domain or openly licensed, logged with license and credit) with the student's own ideas to shape an original piece. MA:Cn10.1.Ib: explain and show how media widens meaning and builds culture, for example through online learning, from the Native Knowledge 360° pages and the class's own collection. Words: blend, audience, collection, program, distribute, culture. Showing: the collection twice, to the younger class and to families. End-of-unit evidence (two check days, the two screenings), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.I, a look-for marked at the first screening: forms blended into one piece with the planned audience moment working; MA:Pr6.1.Ia, the kept collection showing plan: order, two formats and two audiences, how each meets the work; MA:Pr6.1.Ib, a kept written review after the first screening naming one improvement, with the second screening's look-for showing it made, and the gains to self and community weighed; MA:Cn10.1.Ia, the kept source log and credits on the piece: outside sources judged and woven with the student's own (scored at the mid-unit checkpoint on the rough cut); MA:Cn10.1.Ib, a written account on the second check day: how the collection and one online teaching page widen meaning and build culture. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: school-subject content and virtual channels (Level 2). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, a program template, Curio Class's own. Free path: a sheet or wall for a screen, printed programs, a live sound score. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the collection is built from the level's pieces and the new team piece, and the showing ends the level before the year-end check in week 32. The source log, kept since the bridge, is weighed here for the first time | Candidate: the second audience moved to a community partner's space where the school allows it, with every yes | Native Knowledge 360°: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first and the unit uses them as Native educators present them; no student restages a Native practice. Remix: public domain or openly licensed sources only, named on the piece. Showing outside class needs each yes. Hazards and controls: an evening showing in the school's space with the school's own adults; cords taped; no flashing light; headphones low. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
