docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-7/frame.md:147:| 1 | The salmon year. "When the river a family has fished for generations changes, what do they hold on to, and what do they let go?" | 30 | e7-cite-several; e7-theme-development; e7-story-elements-interact; e7-contrast-pov; e7-narrative-writing; e7-reading-range; e7-text-response-writing; e7-collab-discussion; e7-revise-purpose-audience; e7-commas-spelling | RL.7.1; RI.7.1; RL.7.2; RL.7.3; RL.7.6; W.7.3; RL.7.10; RI.7.10; W.7.9; W.7.10; SL.7.1; W.7.5; L.7.3; L.7.2 | Texts: an original Curio Class novella read whole (about 30,000 to 35,000 words, the extra pages read in the home reading; lengthened 2026-11-14 so the band's whole texts climb) on a present-day family that fishes the Klamath River, in the last year before its dams came down, told in alternating chapters by a brother and sister, 13 and 15 (rights: our own). The family is not written as Karuk or Yurok: the Tribes' voices come in their own published words in unit 6. Paired: the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's fisheries pages on Klamath salmon and the US Geological Survey's river-flow pages for the Klamath (public domain, federal works). Independent reading: a long book of the student's choice from the Reading Room shelf or home, logged | Several pieces of evidence gathered across chapters for a point stated and one inferred (RL.7.1), and from the fisheries and river-flow pages (RI.7.1). A theme followed from where it is hinted to where it lands, with an objective summary (RL.7.2); how the river, the fish and the family's choices act on one another (RL.7.3); how the author develops and contrasts the two siblings' views (RL.7.6). The discussion routine: prepared with marked text (SL.7.1a), rules for collegial discussion followed, progress tracked toward specific goals and deadlines, and individual roles defined as needed (SL.7.1b), questions posed that elicit elaboration, and responses to others' questions and comments with relevant observations and ideas that bring the discussion back on topic as needed (SL.7.1c), views changed by evidence and said so (SL.7.1d). The evidence paragraph daily (W.7.9a; W.7.9b on the fisheries pages' claims), short and long (W.7.10). The band and the reading log (RL.7.10, RI.7.10). A narrative told by one of two narrators through the full process: orient with point of view (W.7.3a), dialogue, pacing and description (W.7.3b), transitions for time and setting (W.7.3c), precise and sensory words (W.7.3d), an ending that reflects (W.7.3e); revised for purpose and audience with peers and the teacher (W.7.5) and edited for wordiness (L.7.3a); commas between coordinate adjectives (L.7.2a) and spelling (L.7.2b) in their own drafts | A long shared book first, so several-evidence citing, theme development and the discussion routine are taught on one text everyone has read. It starts the year's second strand, the river and its dam, as story, which unit 6 returns to, so unit 6 reads the dam argument with this knowledge; unit 2's Dust Bowl is the year's one drought, and grade 5 holds the band's one ranch-family drought story. Two narrators carry the point-of-view model on from grade 6 unit 5. Retrieval tasks in the first two weeks: one well-chosen line per point and an objective summary (e6-cite-evidence, e6-theme-summary, e6-plot-episodes), one narrator's view on the model (e6-narrator-pov), the band and the log (e6-reading-range), the evidence paragraph and discussion roles (e6-text-response-writing, e6-collab-discussion), a narrative sample revised (e6-narrative-writing, e6-revise-edit), setting off an extra element (e6-punctuation-spelling) | Candidate: the novella's text record (RUBRIC-ELA 2b); the discussion look-for sheet (2i); an end task joining reading and writing on the theme (2v) | Money worries, a closed salmon season and a grandparent's illness; no death on the page. Students from fishing and farm families may be in the room and are not asked to share their own. The novella gets the text agent's read and the sensitivity read, after the Karuk and Yurok study the brief names, since the river is theirs |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-7/frame.md:150:| 4 | Athens: who could speak? "How did Pericles want the world to see Athens, and who did his picture leave out?" | 22 | e7-author-position; e7-argument-writing; e7-text-vs-film | RI.7.6; W.7.1; RL.7.7; RI.7.7 | Texts: anchors (rights: our own): an original feature on the Athenian assembly and who counted as a citizen; an original historical story of a potter's son at his first assembly, with a course-made audio version and a course-made short film of one scene (deliberate camera, lighting and sound choices; the paper path gives stills and a shot list); two original editorials on choosing a youth council, one of them also course-recorded as a speech at a town meeting. Paired primary excerpts, short and read with full support (public domain): Pericles' Funeral Oration in Thucydides (Benjamin Jowett's translation, 1881); Aristotle's Athenian Constitution (F. G. Kenyon's translation, 1891); Theodore Roosevelt, "The Right of the People to Rule" (1912), the sound recording (fixed before 1923) with its transcript, optional | The authors' points of view and purposes, and how each sets their position apart from others (RI.7.6): taught on the two editorials and the feature, then applied to Pericles praising Athens against the feature showing who was left out. The story read, then heard in its audio version and watched in its filmed scene: what each medium's techniques (sound, voices and pace; lighting, color, camera focus and angle) do (RL.7.7); the recorded editorial read and then heard: how delivery changes the words' impact (RI.7.7), with the 1912 recording as an optional second example. A short argument in one sitting first, on Pericles' claim. Then an argument through the full process, "Should our town choose its youth council by lot, as Athens chose most officials?": claim introduced, others acknowledged, reasons organised (W.7.1a), relevant evidence from accurate sources (W.7.1b), words, phrases and clauses that create cohesion and clarify the relationships among claims, reasons and evidence (W.7.1c), formal style (W.7.1d), a conclusion (W.7.1e); typed, about two pages. The story is also read against the feature: what it kept and invented (RL.7.9, used again) | Beside social studies grade 7 unit 8 (days 87 to 100 plus held day 120), which asks whether Athens was a democracy; ELA reads how one speech made the case, and its opening feature teaches the assembly and citizenship before the story or the excerpts need them. Argument comes before social studies unit 12 (about day 145), which asks for it. Author's position after unit 3, where authors' shaping was taught; the year's seminar on Pericles uses SL.7.1c's elaboration questions again. Retrieval tasks: author's purpose and a traced argument (e6-author-purpose, e6-trace-argument), the grade 6 argument plan (e6-argument-writing), reading against hearing and watching (e6-read-vs-watch) | Candidate: the argument rubric used all year (RUBRIC-ELA 2p); a speaking look-for (2j) | Athens excluded women, enslaved people and foreigners from the assembly; stated plainly. The youth-council question is about a town's choice, not a school election, and no student is asked to campaign. The 1912 speech is a campaign speech; students are told so |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-6/frame.md:159:| 1 | River town: the flood year. "What does a river town keep, and what does it change, after a flood?" | 32 | e6-cite-evidence; e6-theme-summary; e6-plot-episodes; e6-reading-range; e6-text-response-writing; e6-collab-discussion; e6-narrative-writing; e6-revise-edit; e6-pronouns; e6-produce-publish | RL.6.1; RI.6.1; RL.6.2; RL.6.3; RL.6.10; RI.6.10; W.6.9; W.6.10; SL.6.1; W.6.3; W.6.5; L.6.3; L.6.1; W.6.4; W.6.6 | Texts: an original Curio Class novella read whole (about 25,000 to 30,000 words, about 16 short chapters, installments have a complete in-school route; the demand, not length, must climb from grade 5's 30,000 to 40,000-word novel. Both texts are unwritten and unmeasured: compare matched passages for syntax, vocabulary, implied meaning, structure and knowledge burden plus a usable band measure before teaching. Retain this length provisionally; choose a harder or longer work only if the finished comparison shows demand does not climb): a present-day hero's trials: a twelve-year-old whose family has lived in the river town for generations and must decide, the spring after a flood, whether to rebuild on the low ground or move uphill, told in the third person (rights: our own). Paired myth: Yu the Great, who tamed China's floods by digging channels where his father's dams had failed, retold in our own words by Curio Class from James Legge's translation of the Shoo King (1865, public domain), about 1,200 words. Also paired: two short US Geological Survey and National Weather Service flood explainers with a flood map, captions and headings (public domain, federal works). Independent reading: a long book of the student's choice from the Reading Room shelf or home, logged | The novella read chapter by chapter: citing one well-chosen line for a point the text states and for one the reader infers (RL.6.1), and citing from the explainers' headings, captions and map (RI.6.1); a theme traced through particular details, and an objective summary with no "I liked" (RL.6.2); the plot episode by episode with a character-change line under the story map (RL.6.3). The Yu retelling read beside the novella for how each hero meets the river's trials (grade 5's theme from how characters respond, in review). The discussion routine taught with frames: come having read and marked the text (SL.6.1a), agreed rules, goals and deadlines, roles (SL.6.1b), questions answered with elaboration (SL.6.1c), key ideas reviewed and others' views paraphrased (SL.6.1d). The daily evidence paragraph: point, quoted line, explanation, on the novella (W.6.9a) and on the explainers (W.6.9b's first use; tracing an argument comes in unit 2), short and long (W.6.10). The band named; the oral reading check and the reading log set up (RL.6.10, RI.6.10). A story in which a character makes a choice the river forces on them, through the full process: orient the reader with narrator and characters (W.6.3a), dialogue, pacing and description (W.6.3b), transitions that signal a shift in time or setting (W.6.3c), precise and sensory words (W.6.3d), an ending that follows (W.6.3e); planned against task, purpose and audience (W.6.4); peer and teacher feedback, revision and editing (W.6.5); sentence patterns varied by combining sentences from the novella and the draft (L.6.3a) and one style and tone kept (L.6.3b); pronouns in their own drafts: case (L.6.1a), intensive pronouns (L.6.1b), shifts in number and person (L.6.1c), vague antecedents (L.6.1d), and variations from standard English identified in their own and others' writing and speech (the novella's dialogue, a recorded exchange), which form fits a named setting explained, and a model, reference or checklist used to revise chosen examples into conventional English (e); typed, about two pages (grade 5's end, unscored retrieval), with peer comments (W.6.6 begins) | A long shared book first, so citing, summary, discussion and the evidence paragraph are taught on one text everyone has read. It picks up grade 5's water unit as story (too much water, a town sharing the cost), and carries Cory's heroes-and-trials decision (a present-day hero, a myth retold as the pair). Grade 5's unit 1 began a family over in a new place; this one stays and chooses. Pronouns sit in the first process piece's revise days, as the row asks. First-use retrieval in the opening lessons: quoting accurately and theme from responses (e5-quote-accurately, e5-theme-response, e5-compare-characters), grade 5 spring reading records with missing/flagged follow-up (e5-reading-range, e5-fluency), discussion roles (e5-discussion), and a one-paragraph narrative sample with its revision (e5-narrative, e5-writing-tasks, e5-writing-process, e5-grammar). A word list is kept from the novella and the explainers as grade 5's e5-academic-words in use | Candidate: the text record for the novella (RUBRIC-ELA 2b, four headings of what makes it hard); the discussion look-for sheet (2i); an end task joining reading and writing on the theme with scoring guidance (2v) | A flooded home, money worries and a hard choice about moving: students who have lived through a disaster may be in the room, so no student is asked to share their own; no death on the page. The Yu retelling is read as a story from a living tradition, not as quaint, and gets the sensitivity agent's read after it studies Chinese sources on the tale (brief). L.6.1e: home languages and dialects are respected and never corrected as wrong in themselves. The novella gets the text agent's read and the sensitivity read |
